   "text": "This is a digoxin-type cardiac glycoside. I assigned the constitution from the drawing and the stereochemistry by comparison with digoxin, so the stereo descriptors are inferred rather than read atom by atom from the wedges.\n\n**Constitution**\n- The aglycone is a steroid with the usual four fused rings, with a methyl on C10 and on C13.\n- There are hydroxyls at C12 and C14.\n- C17 carries a butenolide (2(5H)-furanone) ring.\n- The C3 oxygen is linked to a chain of three 2,6-dideoxy sugars (digitoxose units). Each unit is a pyranose with a ring CH2 next to the anomeric carbon, a 3-OH and a 5-methyl.\n- The two inner sugars are linked through their 4-oxygen. The terminal sugar has free 3-OH and 4-OH groups.\n\n**Stereochemistry**\n- Relative to digoxin's natural configuration, every drawn stereocentre is inverted. The C10 and C13 methyls, the C14-OH and the C17 butenolide are hashed, and the C9-H is wedged, which is the opposite of the natural configuration.\n- I therefore took the digoxin SMILES and inverted every @/@@. I did not independently verify each sugar centre against the wedges, only the steroid ones.\n\nSMILES: C[C@H]1[C@@H]([C@@H](C[C@H](O1)O[C@H]2[C@@H](O[C@@H](C[C@H]2O)O[C@H]3[C@@H](O[C@@H](C[C@H]3O)O[C@@H]4CC[C@@]5([C@H](C4)CC[C@H]6[C@H]5C[C@@H]([C@@]7([C@]6(CC[C@H]7C8=CC(=O)OC8)O)C)O)C)C)C)O)O"
  }
